<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ayacucho</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ayacucho is a city in the central highlands of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and the capital of the region of the same name. The surrounding basin was the heartland of the Wari Empire, whose capital lies nearby. The Spanish founded the city as Huamanga in 1540, and it was renamed after the battle of 1824 that sealed Peruvian independence. In 1980 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a> began its insurgency in the Ayacucho region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ayacucho.jpg" alt="Ayacucho" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marco Chuica Rodríguez · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catedral_de_Ayacucho_(Catedral_Bas%C3%ADlica_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ayacucho lies in the south-central highlands of the Andes, in the valleys of the Huatatas and Chacco rivers. It is the capital of Huamanga Province and of the Ayacucho Region. Its older name, Huamanga, goes back to pre-Hispanic times, and its etymology is obscure. The linguist Rodolfo Cerrón-Palomino derives it from a colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> verb meaning to inaugurate or to come into the light. The name Ayacucho comes from Quechua aya, dead person, and kuchu, corner, and was a common place name in the Andes during the colonial period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Traces of settlement more than 15,000 years old have been found at Pikimachay, about 25 km to the north. The Wari state, which came out of the interaction of the Huarpa and Nasca cultures, came to rule most of the highlands and coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> from a centre near the present city, and was the dominant culture of the Andes before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a>. After its collapse, cities were left and people dispersed into rural hamlets. Colonial chroniclers record a Chanca confederacy, which the Inca defeated.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> founded the Spanish city on 25 April 1540 as San Juan de la Frontera de Huamanga, a stronghold against the attacks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a>. A royal decree of 17 May 1544 titled it the most noble and loyal city. The city was an administrative centre and a stopping place between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>, and a university was founded there in 1677.</p><p>General Juan Antonio Álvarez de Arenales declared independence in Huamanga on 1 November 1820. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ayacucho">Battle of Ayacucho</a> on 9 December 1824, on the pampas of La Quinua nearby, was the last major clash of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peruvian-war-of-independence">Peruvian War of Independence</a>, and the army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a> won it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> renamed the city Ayacucho by decree on 15 February 1825. In 1980 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a> began its campaign in the region, and attacked the Ayacucho prison in 1982; its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abimael-guzman">Abimael Guzmán</a> was captured in 1992.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ayacucho is known as the City of Churches for its 33 colonial churches and for mansions with balconies in the style of Spanish cities, and its Holy Week processions blend European and Andean traditions. The city is regarded as a capital of Peruvian popular art, with altarpieces, carvings in local alabaster known as Huamanga stone, and filigree work, and it has belonged to the UNESCO Creative Cities Network since 2019. Its music includes the yaraví and the huayno, and many songs are sung in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ayacucho” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From Vitcos and Vilcabamba, Manco’s forces attacked Spanish travellers and Andean peoples allied to the Spaniards, such as the Huanca of the Mantaro Valley, and threatened the road between Cusco and Lima; in 1539 the Spaniards founded Huamanga, the modern <b>Ayacucho</b>, partly to guard that road.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-inca-state">Neo-Inca State · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Wari took shape in the <b>Ayacucho</b> basin of the central Peruvian highlands, where farmers of the Huarpa culture had terraced and irrigated the valley slopes during the first half of the first millennium CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/wari-empire">Wari Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Linguists divide it into two main branches: Central Quechua, spoken in the highlands of central Peru, and a second branch that includes the Quechua of northern Peru, Ecuador and Colombia and the Southern Quechua of Cusco, <b>Ayacucho</b>, Bolivia and Argentina.</blockquote><a href="/language/quechua">Quechua · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chanka, or Chanca, occupied the highlands of Apurímac and <b>Ayacucho</b>, west of Cusco, after the collapse of the Wari Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/chanka-confederation">Chanka Confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Andrés de Santa Cruz's Bolivian army defeated his Peruvian opponents, an assembly of the southern departments of Peru, Arequipa, Cusco, Puno and <b>Ayacucho</b>, met at Sicuani in March 1836 and declared the South Peruvian Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/south-peruvian-republic">South Peruvian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
